{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Command the children of Israel.</b> This is the pericope of the commandment of the lamps, and the pericope of \"And you shall command\" (Exodus 27:20) was said only in the context of the work of the Tabernacle, to explain the requirement of the Menorah. Thus is its meaning: and you are eventually going to command the children of Israel concerning this — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 24:2). But it is not correct in my eyes, for that pericope is not adjacent to the pericope of the Menorah, and it was already stated, \"And he lit the lamps before YHWH, as YHWH had commanded Moses\" (Exodus 40:25) — so the commandment and its performance have already been mentioned and carried out. Rather, the need for this pericope is for two things. For there He commanded, \"And they shall bring you pure olive oil from the children of Israel\" — meaning, from any individual who happened to have pure olive oil for the luminary, along with the rest of the contribution for the Tabernacle; and so they did, as stated, \"And the princes brought… the spice and the oil for the luminary\" (Exodus 35:27–28). And although it says there, \"a perpetual statute throughout their generations\" (Exodus 27:21), that refers to the kindling. But now that oil which the princes brought as a donation was exhausted, and He commanded that the children of Israel, for all their generations, must bring pure beaten oil from public funds, like the first oil, in the sense of \"And they shall bring you a red heifer\" (Numbers 19:2) — that they must seek it out and the public must bring it. And there, He said only \"Aaron and his sons shall arrange it,\" and so on, and it could be understood to mean upon the Menorah, or even without a Menorah if it should break or be lost — as was the case when they returned from the exile. But now He explicitly stated, \"upon the pure Menorah\" (Leviticus 24:4) — that they may kindle only upon the pure Menorah. And the midrash of our Rabbis (Sifra, chapter 13:12): \"Upon the pure Menorah\" — for the purity of the Menorah, that they may not prop them with splinters or with wads. \"He shall arrange the lamps before YHWH\" — that he may not prepare them from outside and bring them in. \"Always (תמיד)\" — even on the Sabbath. \"Always (תמיד)\" — even in a state of ritual impurity."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:10.448387223Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}